A.u0. {"problem": "The request says each confirmation should carry a note, but only confirm_element records one. confirm_wording_element (agent) and confirm_all_proposed (owner, bulk) also move elements to confirmed. Neither takes a note or sets confirmation_note. Elements confirmed through them end up with no reason recorded, which defeats the stated purpose.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions confirm_wording_element and confirm_all_proposed; Element.confirmation_note", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u1. {"problem": "confirm_element now has a required `note` param. Every existing caller that confirms without a note is refused or breaks. The interface version in the composition stays at 1 and the domain only moves from version 13 to 14. A required input added to an existing action is a breaking change that should be handled explicitly.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action confirm_element params; toprope-self.composition.yaml, members.authoring interface: 1", "kind": "other"}
A.u2. {"problem": "The new note is mandatory but has no check on content, so an empty or whitespace string would satisfy it. The 'why' the owner agreed can then be blank. Nothing guarantees the field holds a real reason.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, confirm_element params.note", "kind": "other"}
A.u3. {"problem": "The `set` of confirmation_note runs before the kernel call to confirm_element and before record_confirmation. If the kernel refuses or fails the confirmation, the note may still be stored on an element that is not confirmed. The note and the confirmation are not tied together.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, confirm_element effects order", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u4. {"problem": "The action description still reads 'Confirm a drafted element. Only a person confirms.' and the glossary entry for element says nothing about a note. Neither mentions the new requirement. The action's source cites ADR-0037, which does not cover recording a reason, so the new behaviour has no confirming source.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, confirm_element description and source; glossary.element", "kind": "reference"}
A.u5. {"problem": "The request says to keep a note on each confirmation, but only confirm_element records one. confirm_wording_element (an agent confirms wording-only elements) and confirm_all_proposed (bulk confirm through the same kernel confirm_element call) also bring elements to confirmed. They take no note and set no confirmation_note, so those elements end up confirmed with no reason on record. This is the case the change was meant to cover, and the stated reason (later readers want to know why an element was accepted) is not met for them.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions confirm_wording_element and confirm_all_proposed (versus confirm_element)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u6. {"problem": "The new param `note` is required. Before this change, confirm_element took no params, so every existing caller (UI, agent tooling, scripts) that confirms without a note now fails. The note is only optional on the Element property, so the action is stricter than the data. The interface version was not changed to reflect this (the member interface stays 1 and only the file version was bumped).", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action confirm_element params.note; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring interface", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u7. {"problem": "The edited confirm_element action keeps `status: confirmed` and its old source (ADR-0037 L8-L25). That record predates the note and says nothing about it, so the new param and effect carry confirmed status with no backing from the owner. The new Element.confirmation_note property also has no backing. This is a change to a confirmed element that should be re-proposed and re-confirmed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action confirm_element source/status", "kind": "other"}
A.u8. {"problem": "The note is set before the kernel call, and nothing checks that it is non-empty. An empty string satisfies the type, so the 'why' can be blank. The note is also not passed to the kernel, so the stored confirmation (record_confirmation) and the working copy carry no reason. It exists only as an Element field.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, confirm_element effects (set target.confirmation_note) and the call to confirm_element", "kind": "other"}
A.u9. {"problem": "The request says each confirmation keeps a note, but only confirm_element now records one. confirm_wording_element (agent) and confirm_all_proposed (owner) both call the kernel's confirm_element without setting confirmation_note. Elements confirmed through them end up confirmed with no note, which defeats the stated purpose for readers who want to know why an element was accepted. The 'optional: true' on the property hides the gap.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml actions confirm_wording_element and confirm_all_proposed; Element.confirmation_note", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u10. {"problem": "The note is a required param but nothing checks that it is meaningful. An empty string satisfies it, so confirmations can still carry no real reason. No invariant or precondition ties a confirmed Element to a note. The `set` runs before the kernel call, so if the call is refused the note stays on an element that is still proposed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml action confirm_element (params.note, effects order)", "kind": "other"}
A.u11. {"problem": "confirm_element gained a required param, which breaks existing callers that pass none. The authoring interface is still 1 in the composition, and the composition version is unchanged. Anything built against the old signature will be rejected on deploy.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml confirm_element params; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring interface", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u12. {"problem": "The change edits a confirmed action and adds a new field, but neither carries a source or an owner decision for the note requirement. They still cite the old ADR-0037 record, so the new rule isn't traceable to the request.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml actions.confirm_element source; Element.confirmation_note", "kind": "other"}
A.u13. {"problem": "The request says each confirmation should carry the owner's reason, but only confirm_element records a note. confirm_all_proposed also confirms elements (through the same kernel confirm_element call) and takes no note, so those elements are confirmed with no reason. confirm_wording_element confirms elements with no note either. 'Each confirmation' is therefore not met, and later readers cannot tell a confirmation with no note from one made by a bulk or agent path.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml actions confirm_all_proposed and confirm_wording_element; Element.confirmation_note", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u14. {"problem": "confirm_element now has a new required param (note). Every existing caller must supply it, and the interface in toprope-self.composition.yaml stays at 1. Only the domain version was bumped, 13 to 14, which does not signal the breaking change. confirm_element on an element in the unknown state also now needs a note. A required param may be too strict for existing flows; the scenario was updated, but other clients and the 1.x commits and fixtures are not.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml action confirm_element params.note; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring interface", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u15. {"problem": "The note is set in an effect that runs before the kernel confirm_element call. If the kernel call refuses or fails, the element keeps a confirmation_note although it was never confirmed. The note can then say why the owner agreed to something that is still proposed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml confirm_element effects ordering (set before call)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u16. {"problem": "The note is a required string with no minimum, so an empty or blank note passes. The change aims to record the reason for each confirmation, but nothing enforces that a reason is given. No check or invariant ties confirmation_note to review == confirmed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml confirm_element params.note / Element.confirmation_note", "kind": "other"}
A.u17. {"problem": "The request says each confirmation should carry a note, but only confirm_element records one. confirm_all_proposed confirms every proposed element with no note. confirm_wording_element, the agent path, also takes no note and sets none. Elements confirmed by those two actions end up with no confirmation_note, so the 'why was this accepted' trail has gaps.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml actions confirm_all_proposed and confirm_wording_element", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u18. {"problem": "The note is only set on the Element object in this domain. It is not passed in the confirm_element call to the kernel (with: paths, approver). The working copy and commit that later readers see therefore never contain the reason. Also, the set runs before the call, so the note is stored even if the kernel does not actually confirm the element.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml action confirm_element, effects", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u19. {"problem": "The new required param 'note' is a bare string with no non-empty check. An owner can confirm with an empty note, which defeats the purpose. The existing precondition style would allow a check such as params.note != ''.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml action confirm_element, params.note", "kind": "other"}
A.u20. {"problem": "Adding a required param to an existing action changes its call shape. Existing callers of confirm_element, such as clients and agents, will fail without the note. The composition still pins authoring at interface 1, and only the domain version is bumped (13 to 14). A breaking change is shipped without an interface change, and the new param and field have no source or status.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml confirm_element; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other"}

