C.u5. {"problem": "The `confirm_reason` note is written as an effect before the kernel `confirm_element` call. The kernel call is asynchronous and confirmation only lands through `record_confirmation`. If the kernel refuses or fails, the element carries a 'why it was accepted' note while it is still proposed. Nothing ties the note to an actual confirmation, and no invariant requires confirmed elements to have a reason.", "where": "SKILL.md, confirm_element and confirm_wording_element effects; Invariants (Authoring)", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u6. {"problem": "Step 1 of the scenario calls `confirm_wording_element` on E1 without the now-required `reason` param, yet expects denial by precondition `wording-only`. The call is now malformed, so it may be rejected for the missing param instead. The scenario no longer isolates the precondition it is meant to test.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-confirms-only-wording-fixes, step 1", "kind": "reference"}
C.u7. {"problem": "The request is to keep a note on each confirmation. `confirm_all_proposed` also confirms elements, via kernel `confirm_element` with paths='all'. It takes no reason and never sets `confirm_reason`. Elements confirmed this way end up confirmed with no note, which is the gap the change was meant to close. The `confirm_reason` field is optional and no invariant requires it on confirmed elements, so nothing flags the gap.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring action confirm_all_proposed; Element.confirm_reason; Invariants (Authoring)", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u8. {"problem": "The request says the note records why the owner agreed. `confirm_wording_element` is taken by an agent, so its reason is the agent's rationale, not the owner's, and it is stored in the same field. Later readers cannot tell whose reasoning they are reading. The action's own text calls it why 'it was accepted', which is inconsistent with the owner framing.", "where": "SKILL.md, confirm_wording_element effects; Element.confirm_reason", "kind": "other"}
C.u9. {"problem": "The Element description and the glossary entry for 'element' still say elements are drafted and confirmed, with no mention of the new reason. The new required `reason` param is also not reflected in the fixtures. Seeded confirmed elements such as E2 have no `confirm_reason`, which contradicts the 'each confirmation has a note' intent.", "where": "SKILL.md, Element object description, Glossary (Authoring), World fixtures (Authoring) E2", "kind": "reference"}
C.u10. {"problem": "`confirm_wording_element` now has a required `reason` param, but step 1 of the example `an-agent-confirms-only-wording-fixes` still calls it without a reason. It expects denial by precondition `wording-only`. A missing required param may be rejected first for a different cause, so the expected denial reason is no longer reliable. The step needs a reason, or the example must say which check fires first.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-confirms-only-wording-fixes, step 1", "kind": "reference"}
C.u11. {"problem": "The request is to keep a note on each confirmation. `confirm_all_proposed` also confirms elements, via kernel `confirm_element` with paths='all'. It takes no reason and sets no `confirm_reason`, so elements confirmed in bulk end up confirmed with no note. This breaks the stated purpose that later readers can see why an element was accepted. `record_confirm_all` also records nothing about a reason.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring action confirm_all_proposed (and record_confirm_all)", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u12. {"problem": "`confirm_reason` is set before the kernel `confirm_element` call, and `record_confirmation` is the only confirmation of success. If the kernel refuses or fails, the element carries a 'why the owner agreed' note while still unconfirmed. Nothing ties `confirm_reason` to `review == confirmed`, and no invariant checks that a confirmed element has a reason. The reason is also just a required string, so an empty value is accepted. The note can therefore be missing or misleading.", "where": "SKILL.md, Element.confirm_reason; confirm_element and confirm_wording_element effects; Invariants (Authoring)", "kind": "other"}
C.u13. {"problem": "Elements already confirmed before this change, such as fixture E2 (confirmed), have no `confirm_reason`. The property is optional, so they stay without a note. The confirm transitions from `confirmed` are n/a, so a reason cannot be added later. The change request's guarantee of a note on each confirmation does not hold for them.", "where": "SKILL.md, World fixtures (Authoring) E2; Transitions Element confirmed", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u14. {"problem": "Step 1 of this example has the agent take `confirm_wording_element` on E1 with no params. The action now requires `reason (string)`, which is not optional. The expected result, denial by precondition `wording-only`, is no longer reliable, because the call could be rejected for the missing parameter instead. The example should pass a reason so it tests the precondition.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-confirms-only-wording-fixes, step 1", "kind": "reference"}
C.u15. {"problem": "The change asks for a note on each confirmation, but `confirm_all_proposed` also confirms elements. It calls `confirm_element` with paths='all', takes no reason, and sets no `confirm_reason`. Elements confirmed in bulk therefore have no note, and the rule that every confirmation carries a reason does not hold. Nothing requires a confirmed element to have a `confirm_reason`, so the gap goes unnoticed.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring Actions, confirm_all_proposed (and record_confirm_all)", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.u16. {"problem": "One field, `confirm_reason`, holds both the owner's reason from `confirm_element` and the agent's reason from `confirm_wording_element`. The request describes the note as 'why the owner agreed', but an agent-written note can now sit in the same field and look like the owner's. A later reader cannot tell who gave the reason. Nothing records who gave it, and the Element description and glossary still say only that a person confirms.", "where": "SKILL.md, Element.confirm_reason; confirm_wording_element effects; Glossary 'element'", "kind": "other"}
